#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541160} :activity/id #uuid "9d467660-a98d-5edb-bc42-02dab1e4b3ea" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049184 :topic/id #uuid "e1a5b1bc-c302-556f-adf4-38f31ef47d34" :topic/math-academy-id 4144} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541160 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c3047a2-d5df-5a0c-ab0c-a0f194bdad31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}y+2{y}^{3}-6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278851} {:answer/id #uuid "332e5ac0-126d-59e7-a343-89d3450f7bb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279037} {:answer/id #uuid "4371b2b7-1b47-5452-b1ab-806e3857aeef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279196} {:answer/id #uuid "ab87156c-d08c-577f-aff1-abc00dced344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280110} {:answer/id #uuid "e2408eb1-f6f7-5772-bd58-8e40f81dd72f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}y+2{y}^{3}-8y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279196} :answer-field/id #uuid "27a2ccf4-8749-57d6-a76d-0242f177c147" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ca709c99-e06d-5a3f-baf0-9520bf4a14bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-161830" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd18335a2034613658c65186c4fc6e3e74067e730183d3e66fbacf8ab3601928.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,z,\\,-y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,R=-y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-2x)-(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-2y)+(-y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,z,-y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0fb559ef-7e8d-533b-b198-27c986ee0421" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541194}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541178 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b802cb74-ee56-571a-ba2b-74472252f1f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405078} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d7781c-10a9-5713-ab76-29cfaffd54d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-xy\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405079} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6025cd-45c8-596b-b893-c5fd2b0d6043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405080} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f9eadd-2663-5edf-a933-30b97b13faa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405081} {:answer/id #uuid "11453532-2079-5fc7-a118-668744b23d18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405082}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405080} :answer-field/id #uuid "51599c73-a498-5c6e-99ec-3daf9c417dd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1fddd3a8-32de-55ef-b058-e60e47b22c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-161897" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6af3d84594e2b2648dcbbae05a64f03ef5a38c76f5a1ed835fc4c6b6f50ef97a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,-x,\\,-z⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwise where defined, before integration?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=3-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=-x,\\,R=-z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-0\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-(-x)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,-x,-0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A+0 \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4db3fcce-be9d-5f42-a907-5f887c87d7cd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541203}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541184 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05c9ad26-77bc-5b7f-828d-2c0cdf073e56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}+4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278645} {:answer/id #uuid "1152644c-483e-593c-b840-d414f08e1c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278753} {:answer/id #uuid "4268c067-2778-56fa-ab8e-30b1e87b9f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279189} {:answer/id #uuid "59529013-f848-5ed5-a847-a57d0ae0ecf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-5x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279411} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6a9216-3456-56b0-978c-fded9c69ed78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280814}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280814} :answer-field/id #uuid "8306574e-b0dd-58d3-90c0-2fcec5bd2691" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ca856459-2199-5fb8-97c9-ba0974b5b2db" :question/math-academy-id "q-161905" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5$ and the plane $z=0.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,0,y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5,\\,Q=0,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)\\cdot (2x)-(0)\\cdot (2y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+10x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "59d7191b-c0b7-517e-bf2f-a21f70175ce1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541194 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d747dec-4db3-5966-9a2d-772570a4c2a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278717} {:answer/id #uuid "2b49f721-d34f-57b8-887a-a07af8acd027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}-6x{y}^{2}-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278976} {:answer/id #uuid "3db364b4-51c0-5078-8edc-2aab3af9d03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279134} {:answer/id #uuid "6a21fb2b-62f4-563c-8f2f-7175d9195a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}-4x{y}^{2}+10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279552} {:answer/id #uuid "7a5aa06e-86ef-5b2a-8470-8113b5d11a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279682}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279682} :answer-field/id #uuid "99a942db-f0af-510b-bd6b-aeb36a731348" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fca4156b-f8d5-50fa-a7b9-3894a3eed862" :question/math-academy-id "q-161895" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e374f18d8b87de2fddd9a2127ee84709746c7c1b75083441afcc7b6c79402fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨-z,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)\\cdot (-2x)-0\\cdot (-2y)+x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x+x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-9x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-z,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-9x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7d9afcbf-aa91-5aa1-9258-47bfadefbc7d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541178}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541203 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46218ce4-ea58-5de1-86c0-d1c69e873ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279220} {:answer/id #uuid "4f46e641-3ee3-5376-98dd-a02b81e26818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279312} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9b5b14-4c9b-524f-9436-0ac569357f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{xy-y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279717} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d59bc9-b571-564a-bff6-8c35092d9426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x+y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280086} {:answer/id #uuid "cc67d8e2-c014-5128-9067-69e0d0ab8a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280386}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280386} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ddaedb2-b68c-5509-aca4-917bce34c4eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a4e1f048-4c99-5435-999b-508c95c11d59" :question/math-academy-id "q-161899" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a48be0b4c101babf157a05959e923576ad5614113b3eec9e93fe747d5dc3803.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,x,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=x,\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨1,x,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A+0 \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "91e8a20b-e6de-543d-ae33-ca160809893a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541184}}] :activity/title "Calculating Flux Through Closed Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540522}]}
